<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_faraday-challenge" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Challenge - Magnetic Induction</title>
	
	<!--
	If you want an A*R*C*S (Analyze-Represent-Calculate-Sensemake) activity, use the <project> tag.
	If you want an Explain activity, use the <investigation> tag.
		Use this text for qualitative comparisons: <em>greater than</em>, <em>less than</em>, or <em>equal to</em>
	If you want a Calculate activity, use the <exploration> tag.
	
	<m># \mathrm{~}</m>
	<m> \mathrm{~}</m>
	-->


<investigation>
		<title>Expanding Loop</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			
			<p>
				The figure below shows a circular wire loop in a region with a uniform outward magnetic field.  Suppose the loop has a radius that is <em>increasing</em> in time!
			</p>
			<figure xml:id="fig-loop-expand">
				<caption>A circular wire loop in a uniform magnetic field.</caption>
				<image source="Loop-Expand.png" width = "50%" ><description>A circular loop in a region with outward magnetic field.</description></image>
			</figure>
			<p>
				Determine the direction of the current induced in the loop using both Lenz's Law and the Lorentz Force law.
			</p>
		</statement>
		
	</investigation>



		<project>
		<title>Charging a Phone</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<p>
				You have a circular coil of radius <m>25 \mathrm{~cm}</m> with <m>200 \mathrm{~turns}</m> and resistance <m>5 \mathrm{~\Omega}</m> that you would like to use to generate some power for charging your phone. You look up and see that <m>9 \mathrm{~m}</m> above you is a long, straight power line that has a current with frequency <m>f = 50 \mathrm{~Hz}</m> given by:
				<me>I(t) = \left(700 \mathrm{~A}\right) \sin\left( 2\pi ft\right)</me>
				Estimate the maximum value of the current through your coil.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</project>

	

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Metacognitive Reflection</title>
		<activity>
		<title>Induced Current</title>
		<statement><p></p>
			<p>
				Write a short paragraph discussing how a changing magnetic field can result in an induced current. As part of your description, highlight specific activities you completed this week.
			</p>
		</statement>
			
		</activity>
		
	</subsubsection>

	

<!--NO AI-->
</section>